{"sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","type":"heading","text":"The Snapshot Pattern"},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"Store.send()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via ","type":"text"},{"code":"SwiduxObservable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"text":" packs, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"text":" unpacks.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" equality checking.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accessor checks ","type":"text"},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"_modify","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor (used by "},{"code":"inout","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through "},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Cross-slice observation isolation.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates a separate "},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" class for each "},{"code":"@Slice","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" property. A view reading "},{"code":"store.items","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" won’t re-render when only "},{"code":"store.ui","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects."}]}]}],"type":"orderedList"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" macro generates the observer class tree and ","type":"text"},{"code":"SwiduxObservable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit equality guards ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }"},{"type":"text","text":") are unnecessary. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" already checks equality on ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Unconditional assignment is safe.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside","name":"Note"},{"anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","level":2,"text":"Dispatch Loop Detection","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" warns if ","type":"text"},{"code":"afterReduce","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is called more than 100 times per debounce interval. This usually means ","type":"text"},{"code":"send()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" isn’t using the snaps